          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"rostand-cyrano-de-bergerac-ca838af570a46ee9\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Christian's direct “Je t'aime” is principally an avowal presenting his love for Roxane as true. Nothing in the utterance independently undertakes a commitment, and the deliberate courtship exchange provides no evidence that the words are a reflex-like affective discharge rather than an emotionally invested assertion.\",\"ambiguity\":\"Christian's desire and limited eloquence may complicate the depth or character of his feelings, but not the utterance's strongly truth-conditional mode.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Christian directly addresses Roxane with “Je t'aime” during their face-to-face courtship conversation.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Christian has rejected Cyrano's help and deliberately attempts to speak for himself. Roxane accepts that he loves her but demands an eloquent account of how he loves her; thus the comic failure concerns his inability to elaborate, not the semantic force of this avowal. The exclamation mark and romantic intensity do not independently establish expressive/reflexive force, while the courtship setting alone does not make the utterance a relational undertaking.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“CHRISTIAN: Je t'aime !”\",\"supports\":\"The first-person present-tense declaration directly presents Christian's love for Roxane as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately before the exchange Christian says he is tired of borrowing letters and speeches and will “parler moi-même”; after a silence he begins declaring his love.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance is a deliberate personal avowal rather than a prompted quotation or involuntary verbal reflex, supporting high T and E=0.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Roxane replies, “C'est le thème. Brodez, brodez,” and later “Vous m'aimez, je sais.”\",\"supports\":\"Roxane treats the proposition that Christian loves her as established but finds its expression insufficiently elaborated; this supports an assertive/avowal reading rather than P or O.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-tense explicit love expression in Edmond Rostand's Cyrano de Bergerac.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the classified occurrence is the direct affirmative love declaration in the supplied French source.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T adequately captures the core avowal. Its romantic intensity, exclamatory punctuation, and comic inadequacy are contextual or delivery features and create no residual pressure for E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
